KNOWNBUG libcxx
main.cpp
--cpp11 --stdlib libc++
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
too many addressed objects
no body for callee
--
libc++ defines std::function<int()>'s members outside the class, in the
partial specialization function<_Rp(_ArgTypes...)>, and an empty pack
loses them (cpp11_partial_specialization_member_defined_outside_empty_pack):
the call returns a nondeterministic value.  Until virtual tables that no
reachable code uses were dropped before function pointer removal, the
call ~__value_func makes through __base::destroy() also had about 400
candidate targets and exceeded the default --object-bits.  With libstdc++
the program verifies in under a second.
